    "summary": "First live Ripple & Brokedown Palace; tight acoustic‑electric flow",
    "blurb": "The show delivers historic premieres of Ripple, Brokedown Palace, Operator and Truckin', with a brilliant acoustic set that flows into a high‑energy electric second set featuring Cryptical Envelopment > The Other One and Attics Of My Life. Jerry’s guitar tone and vocal presence stand out throughout, and the band’s tight interplay makes this a must‑listen early‑’70 performance.",
    "review": "On August 18, 1970 at Fillmore West the Dead offered a landmark concert that introduced several songs that would become staples of their catalog. The first set was an acoustic marvel – Jerry Garcia’s guitar shone on the debut of Ripple and Brokedown Palace, while his vocal presence carried Operator and New Speedway Boogie with effortless grace. The electric second set exploded into a soaring Cryptical Envelopment > The Other One sequence, then rode a powerful wave through Attics Of My Life, It’s A Man’s World and a rousing finish with Uncle John’s Band. Across all recordings reviewers praised the historic significance of hearing these songs for the first time live, noting Jerry’s exceptional tone and his multi‑instrumental contributions, while the rhythm section kept a tight, propulsive drive.\n\nAmong the available sources the matrix recording (gd1970-08-18.141071.aud.pitch-adjusted.flac1644) stands out as the definitive version: an excellent multi‑source mix that captures both the intimate acoustic ambience and the full‑throttle electric energy without any audible flaws. Whether you’re a completist or just discovering these early gems, this show offers a rare glimpse of the Dead at a creative crossroads, delivering tight playing, historic firsts, and a concert flow that still feels fresh today.",
